                <pb facs="1341" n="1236" sameAs="frus:frus1958-60v03mSupp/pg1236"/>

                <head>291. Record of Telephone Conversation Between John Foster Dulles and Sherman Adams<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d291-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><date calendar="gregorian"
                            type="conversation-or-meeting-date" when="1958-02-19T15:33:00">February
                            19, 1958, 3:33 p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <pb facs="d291-1" n="1" type="facsimile" xml:id="d291-1"/>
                <p>The Sec said he had quite a talk with Gruenther re disarmament. The Sec suggested
                    the handling of it should be under the control of State through some regular
                    fellow but that we should have an outside panel of advisers or consultants to
                    guide us. G thought that was a good way and he would be agreeable to serving on
                    such a panel. The Sec thought <pb facs="1342" n="1237" type="typeset"
                        xml:id="pg1237"/> of Bedell Smith
                    and he recommended Professor Wolfers of Yale. Does this commend itself to A? A
                    said yes—he should think so. A said if you get a panel you might as well put it
                    in the newspapers so you might as well get good names. Get whom you want. The
                    Sec said this is largely scenery. It is a public relations job and you are never
                    going to get anywhere with these…… A said to get some practical people who can
                    sit around and talk about it. A asked what about CD? He does not want to help?
                    The Sec indicated such was the case.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
